Build a standalone, locally runnable Kubernetes Deployment Readiness utility by reusing the existing Release Tracker UI and backend logic wherever possible. Do not host this utility inside the main AADLC application.

1. Standalone Tool

* Package the utility as a downloadable ZIP containing the UI, Python scripts, dependencies, setup/run instructions, and required configuration templates.
* Developers should be able to extract the ZIP, configure their local settings, and run it on their machines.
* Use a local SQLite database for temporary storage. Create and initialize it automatically when required.
* Keep the implementation simple; reuse existing working logic rather than rebuilding it unnecessarily.

2. Connectivity

* Support the existing Kubernetes private-link connectivity, credentials/secrets, cookies, and dashboard API URLs.
* Provide a simple connection-configuration UI with a Test Connection option.
* Reuse existing authentication and connectivity logic wherever possible.
* Do not package or distribute private links, tokens, cookies, or secrets in the ZIP.
* Users who have the required private-link access and credentials should be able to configure them locally.
* Keep dashboard API connectivity as the alternative/default path for users who do not have Kubernetes access, wherever supported by the existing APIs.
* Never log or expose credentials or cookies in the UI, logs, or SQLite database.

3. Deployment Readiness UI

Reuse the existing, currently disabled Kubernetes UI and functionality.

The page should provide:

* Team dropdown.
* Fix Version dropdown.
* Ability to fetch stories for the selected team and fix version.
* Component selection based on the fetched stories.
* Deployment readiness information for these five environments:
    * Test 3
    * Test 4
    * Stage
    * Production East
    * Production West

4. Keep the UI Simple

* Display the available components and their relevant branch-cut/deployment readiness status.
* Clearly distinguish Branch Cut Pending from Branch Cut Completed.
* Avoid the extensive report preview, violation details, and other unrelated information shown in the main Release Tracker.
* Keep the interface focused exclusively on Kubernetes deployment readiness.

5. Local Data and Execution

* Store temporary application data in SQLite.
* Reuse existing story-fetching, component-selection, branch-cut, and deployment-readiness logic wherever applicable.
* Keep configuration external to the packaged code so developers can configure their own environments.
* Ensure the utility works independently of the hosted AADLC application, except for the external APIs it explicitly needs.

6. Validation and Delivery

* Verify the complete flow: configure connectivity → test connection → select team and fix version → fetch stories → select components → view readiness across all five environments.
* Test both private-link/Kubernetes connectivity and the supported dashboard API path.
* Verify that a fresh ZIP can be extracted and run locally using the documented instructions.
* Confirm that no secrets are included in the ZIP and that existing main Release Tracker functionality remains unaffected.

Keep the implementation focused and reusable. If any existing API or authentication flow does not support the proposed standalone behavior, identify the limitation before introducing a new mechanism.
